  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which modality (RNA, image, or both), encoder, profile setting, or aggregation strategy performs best. The single most important uncertainty is establishing a reliable performance floor. 2) What this tests: config_001 is the simplest RNA-only baseline (single profile, PCA-2000 encoder, no aggregation/fusion). It gives a clean, reproducible reference for RNA-only performance with a standard, low-variance encoder. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: With a 10-experiment budget, the first run should be a simple, fast, and reliable baseline that anchors all later comparisons. A PCA-based RNA-only model is unlikely to be the final winner, but it provides the critical floor needed to (a) determine whether image-only or multimodal configurations add value, (b) calibrate how much headroom more sophisticated encoders (scvi, scgpt) or multiprofile aggregation offer, and (c) avoid wasting later experiments on configurations that are merely 'complex' rather than genuinely better. Starting simple also minimizes the risk of conflating multiple design changes in a single comparison, keeping the subsequent search interpretable."
